# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules.  [source] [destination] [status]
#
# Evaluated before static assets are served, so a rule here wins over a file of
# the same name.
#
# Rules are matched top to bottom and the FIRST match wins, so anything
# specific has to sit above the catch-all at the bottom of this file.
#
# ── One URL that was never meant to exist ───────────────────────────────────
# https://jaan.io/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md/ is live, indexed,
# and an accident: a file named 2016-07-24-…-perspectives.md.bak sat in the
# Jekyll _posts directory, Jekyll's glob matched it anyway, and it published a
# page whose slug carries the .md through into the URL. What it serves is a cut
# section of the VAE tutorial — three bullets on why the probability-model view
# beats the neural-net one — with no title and no frontmatter.
#
# So it does not become a page here. It sends its traffic to the post the text
# was cut from, which is the nearest thing to what a visitor arriving on it was
# looking for. Both forms are listed rather than letting the slashed one fall
# through to the rule below, which would land it on a bare .md path that has
# nothing behind it: one hop from wherever the link points, not two into a 404.
/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md/ /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/variational-autoencoder-perspectives.md /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301

# ── /blog/ → /articles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The article index is the ONE url this port changes. jaan.io has served the
# list at /blog/ since 2013 and this site calls it /articles — the name the nav
# uses, and the one the posts' own `category: articles` frontmatter already
# said. The individual POST urls are unchanged and must stay that way; only the
# index moves, so only the index redirects.
#
# Both forms are listed rather than letting the slashed one fall through to the
# catch-all at the bottom. That would work, but as TWO hops — /blog/ → /blog →
# /articles — and a redirect chain costs a round trip and leaks ranking signal
# at every link. One hop from wherever the link points.
/blog/ /articles 301
/blog /articles 301

# ── notonspotify ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A clock that tells track time, for music that isn't on Spotify. It lives in
# its own repo on its own subdomain; this is the memorable address people are
# given, and the only line about it in this repo.
#
# 302, not 301: the subdomain is where it happens to live today, and a
# permanent redirect would be a promise this site has no reason to make.
#
# Both forms are listed so the slashed one is a single hop rather than two
# through the catch-all below.
/notonspotify/ https://notonspotify.jaan.io 302
/notonspotify https://notonspotify.jaan.io 302

# ── The VAE tutorial's three old names ──────────────────────────────────────
# The post has been renamed twice, and jaan.io still serves both earlier names.
# Live today, /unreasonable-confusion 301s to /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae,
# which 301s AGAIN to the tutorial — a two-hop chain carried over from
# s3_website.yml, where each rename simply added a rule pointing at the name
# before it. Both are collapsed here: every old name goes straight to the
# tutorial in ONE hop. Nothing points at the middle name any more.
#
# 301, and these are the ones where it genuinely matters. This is the most
# linked-to writing on the site, the chain is load-bearing for its ranking, and
# a hop that is dropped rather than collapsed loses the signal outright.
#
# The `/index.html` twins are not tidiness. S3 static hosting needed a separate
# rule for each, so they were distinct, publicly-reachable, indexable paths for
# a decade. They cost one line each and cannot be recovered later if a crawler
# still holds them. They do NOT fall through to the catch-all at the bottom of
# this file — that rule matches only paths ENDING in a slash, and these end in
# `.html`.
/unreasonable-confusion/index.html /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/unreasonable-confusion/ /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/unreasonable-confusion /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae/index.html /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae/ /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301
/what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae /what-is-variational-autoencoder-vae-tutorial 301

# ── /sitemap.xml → /sitemap-index.xml ───────────────────────────────────────
# jaan.io has served a sitemap at /sitemap.xml since 2013, so that is the path
# Google and Bing already have on file and re-request on their own schedule.
# @astrojs/sitemap emits `sitemap-index.xml` plus `sitemap-0.xml` instead, and
# robots.txt points at the index — so without this line the path both crawlers
# already know starts 404ing at cutover, which Search Console reports as a
# sitemap that has stopped existing rather than as one that moved.
#
# A redirect rather than a copy of the file: two sitemaps at two URLs is two
# things to keep in step, and a 301 is a thing crawlers follow and remember.
/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301

# ── Vanity shortcuts to other people's services ─────────────────────────────
# Typed and spoken more than they are linked — the whole point of them is that
# they are short enough to say out loud. None of them is content this site
# owns; each is a pointer at something living on a service that can move it.
#
# 302, NOT 301, and for the same reason as /notonspotify above: a 301 is cached
# by the browser more or less permanently, so the day the Zoom room or the form
# changes, everyone who has ever followed the old one keeps going to the dead
# destination with no way to clear it. These paths carry no ranking worth
# preserving, so the permanence buys nothing and costs exactly that.
#
# /feedback points at the FORM, not at the goo.gl short link s3_website.yml
# used. That shortener still resolves, but Google has been retiring goo.gl, and
# a link that dies quietly inside a redirect is the failure mode this file
# exists to prevent. One less hop, and one less service in the chain.
/feedback/ https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdAWfrXnmNDyIHGQMqmEK_Uwc4VEgiI7GQ5h3hpK3PDM_UsLg/viewform 302
/feedback https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdAWfrXnmNDyIHGQMqmEK_Uwc4VEgiI7GQ5h3hpK3PDM_UsLg/viewform 302
/zoom/ https://us04web.zoom.us/j/4234569486?pwd=VFgxNG5qaWlWb3ZPNHkzOFVlSzFnUT09 302
/zoom https://us04web.zoom.us/j/4234569486?pwd=VFgxNG5qaWlWb3ZPNHkzOFVlSzFnUT09 302
/guest/ https://www.dropbox.com/s/0qi1xhd4qr8pt80/thinkspace.md?dl=0 302
/guest https://www.dropbox.com/s/0qi1xhd4qr8pt80/thinkspace.md?dl=0 302

# ── Two rules from s3_website.yml that are deliberately NOT here ────────────
# Both would be redirects onto something that no longer exists, which is worse
# than a 404: a visitor waits through a hop to arrive nowhere, and a crawler
# records the destination rather than the dead end.
#
# /impact → a Figma community file, GONE. The old README said curl's 403 was
# probably bot-blocking and to check it in a browser. It is not bot-blocking:
# with a browser User-Agent that file id returns 404 while figma.com/community
# itself returns 200, so the file has been taken down or made private. Restore
# it by pasting this back with a working URL:
#     /impact https://www.figma.com/community/file/<id>/<slug> 302
#
# /dont-become-data-for-AI → the radicalization post, which is `published:
# false` in the Jekyll tree and 404s on jaan.io today. The post is staying
# unpublished, so the redirect goes too, and both forms plus the `/index.html`
# twin now 404 directly instead of 301ing into one.

# ── Trailing slash → no trailing slash ──────────────────────────────────────
# The site serves /about, not /about/ (astro.config.mjs `trailingSlash: never`
# + `format: "file"`, which emits about.html). This is the other half of that
# decision: one canonical URL per page, and one permanent redirect pointing at
# it, so the same content is never reachable at two live addresses.
#
# It is not cosmetic here. jaan.io has served `permalink: /:title/` since 2013,
# so EVERY indexed URL — the ones carrying 20–30k organic visits a month — ends
# in a slash. At DNS cutover this rule is what turns each of them from a 404
# into a 301 onto the new form. Losing it would not look broken in testing (the
# nav links are already slash-less) and would quietly drop the traffic the port
# exists to keep.
#
# 301, not 302: the move is permanent, and only a 301 passes ranking signals on
# to the destination.
#
# The splat cannot swallow the site root. `/*/` requires a slash AFTER whatever
# `*` matches, and "/" has no second slash — so "/" never matches and there is
# no redirect loop. Verified against the deployment, not assumed.
/*/ /:splat 301
